Phasmid: WebGL on hosts without a usable GPU

What:     With a profile, the enable_unsafe_swiftshader local state pref
          defaults to true, so a host whose GPU is missing, blocklisted or
          disabled renders WebGL on SwiftShader instead of offering no
          context. A host with a usable GPU is unchanged. The policy of the
          same name still overrides the default.
Why:      Every profile claims a desktop GPU, but stock Chrome with no usable
          GPU (containers, VMs, Xvfb, --disable-gpu) offers no WebGL context.
Profile:  none (active whenever a profile is loaded)
Hooks:    chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
          (ChromeContentBrowserClient::RegisterLocalStatePrefs)
Contexts: main, same- and cross-origin iframe; workers' OffscreenCanvas
          shares the GPU process.
Guard:    tests/guards/graphics/webgl-fallback.py

--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc
@@ -1603,7 +1603,10 @@
   registry->RegisterIntegerPref(prefs::kSCTAuditingHashdanceReportCount, 0);
   registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kDataURLWhitespacePreservationEnabled,
                                 true);
-  registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kEnableUnsafeSwiftShader, false);
+  // Phasmid: the claimed device has a GPU, so WebGL always exists; a host
+  // without a usable one renders it on SwiftShader instead of offering none.
+  registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kEnableUnsafeSwiftShader,
+                                phasmid::Profile::Get() != nullptr);
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX) || BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
   registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kOutOfProcessSystemDnsResolutionEnabled,
                                 true);
